---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import FileHashChecker from '@/components/tools/security/FileHashChecker.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="file-hash-checker">
  <FileHashChecker client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to verify a download's checksum</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Drop the downloaded file onto the panel above, of any size and type. The tool reads it from your
        disk in slices and does not upload it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Paste the checksum from the publisher. A bare hex string, a whole <code>sha256sum</code> line, a
        BSD-style <code>SHA256 (file) = …</code> line, or a colon-separated fingerprint all work. The tool
        detects the algorithm from the digest length and turns it on.
      </li>
      <li>
        Wait for the pass to finish. The tool shows throughput and progress, and you can cancel at any
        time.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read the result. A match means the file on your disk is byte-for-byte the file the publisher
        hashed. A mismatch means it is not: do not open it.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why an upload-based checksum tool is the wrong idea</h2>
    <p>
      You verify a checksum to find out if a file you do not fully trust is genuine. If you upload that
      file to a website, you give the suspicious bytes to a third party and then trust what it says about
      them. You also upload what can be gigabytes, and you cannot know what happens to the file later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Verification has meaning only where the file already is. This file hash checker reads the file from
      your disk with the File API, hashes it locally, and makes no network requests after it loads. You can
      confirm this in the network panel of your browser while it processes a 10 GB image.
    </p>

    <h2>Which algorithm should you use?</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Algorithm</th>
          <th>Digest size</th>
          <th>Hex characters</th>
          <th>Status</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>MD5</td>
          <td>128 bits</td>
          <td>32</td>
          <td>Broken for security. Collisions are easy to construct. Fine for detecting corruption.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>SHA-1</td>
          <td>160 bits</td>
          <td>40</td>
          <td>Broken since the 2017 SHAttered collision. Legacy checksums only.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>SHA-256</td>
          <td>256 bits</td>
          <td>64</td>
          <td>The current default for release checksums, signatures and integrity checks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>SHA-512</td>
          <td>512 bits</td>
          <td>128</td>
          <td>SHA-2 with 64-bit words. No stronger in practice, often faster on 64-bit CPUs.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The important distinction is between accidental and deliberate change. To catch a download that a bad
      connection truncated, MD5 is enough: a corrupted file does not produce a matching digest by chance. To
      prove that nobody replaced the file with a malicious one, MD5 and SHA-1 are useless, because an
      attacker who controls the file can construct a different file with the same digest. Use SHA-256 when
      the publisher offers it, and treat a project that publishes only MD5 as a mild warning sign.
    </p>

    <h3>A checksum does not prove authenticity on its own</h3>
    <p>
      If the checksum is on the same web page as the download link, an attacker who can replace the file can
      also replace the checksum. Checksums protect against corruption and against a compromised mirror. They
      protect against tampering only when the digest comes from an independent source: a signed release file,
      the signing key of the project, a different domain, or a copy you saved earlier.
    </p>

    <h2>Hashing very large files in a browser</h2>
    <p>
      The native <code>crypto.subtle.digest</code> of the browser is fast but has no streaming interface: it
      needs the whole input in memory at once, so it cannot handle files larger than a few gigabytes. This
      tool uses native digests for small files and its own incremental implementations of MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256
      and SHA-512 for large ones. It feeds them 4 MB at a time and yields to the browser between slices, so the
      page stays responsive.
    </p>
    <p>
      Peak memory stays at a few megabytes for any file size, so the practical limit is the read speed of your
      disk, not your RAM. The implementations are checked against the standard FIPS 180-4 and RFC 1321 test
      vectors, including the difficult cases around block and padding boundaries.
    </p>

    <h2>The checksums do not match: now what?</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Check that you compare the same algorithm. A SHA-256 digest never matches an MD5 one. The tool detects
        the algorithm from the length, but when a publisher lists several files, it is easy to copy the wrong
        line.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check that you have the correct file. Different architectures, editions and point releases have
        different checksums, and browsers sometimes add <code>(1)</code> to a downloaded file instead of
        replacing the old one.
      </li>
      <li>
        Download it again, if possible from a different mirror. Most mismatches come from interrupted or
        truncated downloads, and a second attempt fixes them.
      </li>
      <li>
        If it still does not match, stop. Do not run the installer to "see what happens". Report it to the
        project, and get the file from the official source.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Command-line equivalents</h2>
    <p>
      The results match what the tools of your operating system produce, so you can cross-check:
    </p>
    <pre><code>sha256sum ubuntu-24.04.iso            # Linux
shasum -a 256 ubuntu-24.04.iso        # macOS
certutil -hashfile ubuntu.iso SHA256  # Windows
Get-FileHash ubuntu.iso -Algorithm SHA256  # PowerShell</code></pre>
    <p>
      The digests are identical, because a hash function has exactly one correct answer for a given set of
      bytes. With this page, you do not need to remember which of these four commands your machine uses.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
